1
00:00:05,474 --> 00:00:07,710
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:10,590
Last time, we did the sketch of the concept art.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,390
This time, let's begin the coloring.

4
00:00:14,895 --> 00:00:18,200
First, here's the sketch I worked on last time.

5
00:00:18,201 --> 00:00:22,001
And I've tidied up and modified the sketch a bit.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:28,520
Since I'm the type to keep worrying once something sticks in my mind, I often fix the picture as I draw.

7
00:00:28,521 --> 00:00:32,759
Something subtly bothered me, so I made corrections here and there.

8
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,921
I'll explain the reasons for the corrections before I start.

9
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:42,680
First, I turned the body slightly and gave it a bit more overall perspective.

10
00:00:42,681 --> 00:00:44,680
It was too front-facing, so it looked less interesting.

11
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:48,260
It's not a big correction, just overall.

12
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:54,860
I gave it perspective like this.

13
00:00:59,159 --> 00:01:00,220
The front shoulder here.

14
00:01:02,085 --> 00:01:07,840
I raised it a little and lowered the legs a bit, a simple correction.

15
00:01:08,589 --> 00:01:10,960
Refer back to Chapter 4 for this.

16
00:01:13,589 --> 00:01:16,600
This clothing part, whether it's a coat or a gown.

17
00:01:17,979 --> 00:01:23,230
In the previous sketch, it seemed to lack some three-dimensionality, so I raised the collar.

18
00:01:25,179 --> 00:01:29,790
That way, I made it a more realistic clothing piece.

19
00:01:29,791 --> 00:01:35,070
This clothing is set to be fixed to the arm here so it doesn't fall down.

20
00:01:37,109 --> 00:01:49,281
And these translucent panels, instead of covering everything, I made them cover sparsely, to bring out a bit more of a random, fresh feel.

21
00:01:50,574 --> 00:01:57,921
And the clothing was too reminiscent of a plug suit from Evangelion, so I modified it a bit.

22
00:01:58,914 --> 00:02:03,630
And I removed these elements from the clothing.

23
00:02:03,631 --> 00:02:07,230
Once coloring progresses, the volume of the clothing will be determined.

24
00:02:07,230 --> 00:02:11,431
I judged that it'd be better to work on top of that.

25
00:02:11,899 --> 00:02:16,590
When adding patterns to clothing, I tend to consider the clothing's volume.

26
00:02:16,591 --> 00:02:22,311
In concept art work, due to time constraints, I sometimes unavoidably compose patterns flatly.

27
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:33,340
For example, like this.

28
00:02:33,341 --> 00:02:37,500
You'd draw it like projecting a beam onto a curtain, without considering the clothing folds.

29
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:44,060
It's very convenient and quick to work, but the volume somewhat decreases.

30
00:02:44,730 --> 00:02:49,900
Since this is a personal piece and a demo, I'll draw it while bringing out the volume.

31
00:02:52,444 --> 00:02:56,490
With a few elements reduced, it even feels simpler.

32
00:02:56,491 --> 00:02:59,130
I really like very simple designs.

33
00:02:59,131 --> 00:03:03,011
Putting aside whether it looks good, it's easy to draw.

34
00:03:04,224 --> 00:03:06,990
I still don't draw with references up.

35
00:03:06,991 --> 00:03:11,550
Right now, I need the process of envisioning my design and organizing it in my head.

36
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:18,291
Honestly, I wanted to sketch perfectly and then color, but I'll just start at about this level.

37
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:24,451
I thought it'd be better to show you the way I actually work in practice.

38
00:03:24,575 --> 00:03:28,131
And I'll try to refrain from speeding up the video as much as possible.

39
00:03:28,250 --> 00:03:33,651
I have the thought that I should keep talking, so I was going to speed up the parts where I'm not talking.

40
00:03:33,695 --> 00:03:35,690
But seeing is more important than talking, right?

41
00:03:38,095 --> 00:03:39,800
I've divided the layers like this.

42
00:03:40,149 --> 00:03:43,841
The more it's a top-covering element, the higher I put the layer.

43
00:03:45,954 --> 00:03:51,950
At the very back, there's the coat tail and the hair, and on top of that is the skin layer.

44
00:03:54,364 --> 00:03:58,360
There are clothing layers one and two.

45
00:03:58,361 --> 00:04:00,120
On top of that, there's the front coat tail.

46
00:04:03,825 --> 00:04:07,450
And the collar is on top of that.

47
00:04:09,445 --> 00:04:13,520
I've grouped the details here separately.

48
00:04:13,521 --> 00:04:17,360
When I turn on the details, I'll work with it temporarily off.

49
00:04:17,361 --> 00:04:24,320
I'll flexibly merge and expand these layers, but I won't explain every time.

50
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:32,860
First, let me set the base color.

51
00:04:32,861 --> 00:04:34,300
It's good to try various things.

52
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:39,640
Since I decided to mainly use a blue tone.

53
00:04:41,150 --> 00:04:45,560
I'll draw within a range that doesn't deviate from this.

54
00:04:59,425 --> 00:05:03,900
There's a game where you can freely change the color scheme of clothing.

55
00:05:03,901 --> 00:05:08,620
Like My Hero's Academia, or La Vie en Rose Rock... well, games like that.

56
00:05:08,621 --> 00:05:12,780
Even with the same clothing, just changing the color creates uniqueness.

57
00:05:12,781 --> 00:05:16,380
I think it's a really fun and good concept.

58
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:19,580
We can do that while drawing.

59
00:05:19,581 --> 00:05:22,821
Since the layers are separated, we can change them easily.

60
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:09,280
I'll apply broadly until a color I like comes out.

61
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:23,320
Since you can subtly change it as you draw, it's no big deal.

62
00:06:24,835 --> 00:06:27,710
I don't think you need to overthink it.

63
00:06:33,610 --> 00:06:41,050
Well, let me decide on this as a moderate pick.

64
00:06:41,051 --> 00:06:42,730
First, I have to set up the light.

65
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:52,570
I'll use my typical light setup: strong key light from the right, and a soft fill light from the opposite side.

66
00:06:54,410 --> 00:06:57,690
Let me first paint the clothing, the largest area.

67
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:04,380
It's a desaturated bright color, so it's a hard color to handle.

68
00:07:04,381 --> 00:07:11,381
It's good to paint it moderately dark so the volume shows, but it can't be too dark.

69
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:19,161
Naturally, since it's a white garment, considering the inherent value, it shouldn't look darker than the skin.

70
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:28,010
So when painting a white garment, it's good for the light to come from the side or a semi-side, rather than the front, so the contrast can be strong.

71
00:07:37,150 --> 00:07:39,520
Paint considering the shape of the folds.

72
00:07:55,580 --> 00:08:02,041
Since it's too dark, I used an airbrush to brighten the top overall.

73
00:08:17,335 --> 00:08:20,960
Since it's a fitted garment, I also need to bring out the silhouette of the ribcage.

74
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:27,210
I paint broadly.

75
00:08:27,215 --> 00:08:37,170
Since it's an overall bright garment, the contrast isn't large, so it's a bit tricky, but I need to work hard to bring out subtle contrast so the volume can be felt.

76
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:56,151
For blending that softens the boundaries of the brush, I use the mixer brush tool.

77
00:09:24,885 --> 00:09:31,751
By releasing tension from the hand and using pen pressure to paint, I create a naturally connected volume.

78
00:10:03,165 --> 00:10:06,721
I'll hide the detail layer group and work.

79
00:10:51,735 --> 00:10:56,041
The more you paint, the more the shape takes form and the sense of volume comes alive.

80
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:05,320
Painting the overall volume with special care is very, very important.

81
00:11:05,780 --> 00:11:13,320
When clothing has a lot of details, and you paint considering the details, you often can't pay attention to the volume.

82
00:11:13,321 --> 00:11:17,560
Since I'm not using many colors, this is a picture where I can focus even more on volume.

83
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:21,070
I'll also bring out a sense of thickness.

84
00:12:21,071 --> 00:12:24,830
Let me turn off the line layer and check whether the volume is coming out properly.

85
00:12:27,525 --> 00:12:30,720
I'll paint the skin too, following what we learned.

86
00:12:31,230 --> 00:12:38,080
If you've learned human anatomy, you'd have grasped the form, and you'd be able to paint in line with the light.

87
00:12:38,081 --> 00:12:42,041
When coloring, you need to grasp the curvature of the surface well.

88
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:50,181
Paint the recesses dark and establish the shape of the jaw.

89
00:12:52,975 --> 00:12:57,800
Set the shading inside the cheekbone.

90
00:12:57,801 --> 00:13:13,000
When painting skin, you should pay attention to the H of the HSB sliders. It varies with the background environment, but I set the midtones around eighteen to twenty-two, and when painting the lit areas, I move slightly to the right.

91
00:13:13,001 --> 00:13:18,680
In other words, the midtones are relatively reddish, and the bright parts have a bit of yellow mixed in.

92
00:13:20,250 --> 00:13:23,260
I'll also bring out the shape of the chest muscle.

93
00:13:23,261 --> 00:13:25,260
The armpit area, I should make it dark.

94
00:13:27,450 --> 00:13:32,620
The canvas is flat, but the person on top of it should look three-dimensional.

95
00:13:35,635 --> 00:13:42,501
Since the reflected light we set is blue, when painting the reflected light, I lower the saturation.

96
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:47,710
Because the reflected light's color is the complement of the skin color.

97
00:13:47,711 --> 00:13:50,110
Shall we experiment?

98
00:13:50,111 --> 00:13:55,431
Here, watch the S, saturation part of the HSB sliders.

99
00:13:55,880 --> 00:14:03,240
If you mix this complement color, blue, into the skin and sample it with the eyedropper, you can see the saturation drop.

100
00:14:06,810 --> 00:14:11,141
Same for painting the lit areas, I lower the saturation.

101
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:16,190
Because the skin loses a bit of its own color due to the light's color.

102
00:14:17,285 --> 00:14:23,310
But if it receives a very intense warm-colored light, the skin's saturation rises.

103
00:14:23,311 --> 00:14:24,430
We learned that in Chapter 3.

104
00:14:26,085 --> 00:14:30,960
In this picture, the midtones have higher saturation than the lit areas and shadows.

105
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:37,610
It varies by material, but it's good to keep this rule as a base.

106
00:14:39,770 --> 00:14:44,940
I'll add more red on the boundaries between the lit areas and shadows, or the midtones.

107
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:53,811
This is to express the increase in midtone saturation, the flush and lip hue, and subsurface scattering.

108
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:14,760
I'll paint the hair in line with the light too.

109
00:15:14,761 --> 00:15:18,001
We learned the principle of the highlight ring forming on the head.

110
00:15:18,445 --> 00:15:24,120
I really don't use this color on hair, but during the demo I'm getting to use it.

111
00:15:24,121 --> 00:15:26,360
I paint considering the inherent value.

112
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:31,310
Right now, this would be the intermediate stage here.

113
00:15:54,049 --> 00:15:55,050
I paint broadly.

114
00:15:55,051 --> 00:15:57,130
I'll paint the hands roughly too.

115
00:16:09,445 --> 00:16:13,081
The hand size looks big, so I'll shrink it a bit.

116
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:19,390
While painting the base color like this, you can add to or modify the sketch.

117
00:16:19,925 --> 00:16:24,350
Painting the base color reveals the awkwardness of the sketch, which is good.

118
00:16:24,351 --> 00:16:28,750
So rather than just sketching continuously, it's good to alternate with this base color painting.

119
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:37,790
I keep painting.

120
00:16:37,791 --> 00:16:43,070
I plan to make the front coat tail a somewhat semi-transparent material.

121
00:16:43,071 --> 00:16:47,031
So I'll also paint this leg part hidden behind it.

122
00:17:09,534 --> 00:17:13,210
It's good to paint it darker toward the bottom.

123
00:17:13,211 --> 00:17:16,930
The picture won't float, and it'll have stability.

124
00:17:25,444 --> 00:17:28,240
I'll draw while also bringing out the thickness of the clothing.

125
00:17:49,854 --> 00:17:54,250
Now too, I can see parts to fix, so I'm correcting the sketch.

126
00:17:58,649 --> 00:18:01,861
I wanted to create a flow of lines in a diagonal direction.

127
00:18:26,224 --> 00:18:30,570
When drawing a white garment, if the background is white, the clothing blends into the background.

128
00:18:32,409 --> 00:18:36,101
If you apply color to the background, it's easier to establish the volume.

129
00:18:37,365 --> 00:18:42,590
For concept art, you could also leave an outline to separate the white garment and white background.

130
00:18:44,169 --> 00:18:48,650
Since I plan to draw this picture close to an illustration, I'll add a background.

131
00:18:51,739 --> 00:18:53,790
There's a point to be careful about when adding a background.

132
00:18:54,539 --> 00:18:59,390
As we learned in Chapter 3, you should think of the background as environment.

133
00:18:59,470 --> 00:19:03,630
I made the top dark and the bottom bright.

134
00:19:03,710 --> 00:19:08,190
Because the character is bright on top and dark on the bottom.

135
00:19:08,191 --> 00:19:11,689
Using contrast, you can separate the character and background.

136
00:19:14,169 --> 00:19:18,620
Since the background got darker, it's good to darken the figure somewhat too.

137
00:19:18,621 --> 00:19:21,820
I lower the character's value with a curve adjustment.

138
00:19:21,821 --> 00:19:24,060
The curve adjustment shortcut is Control and M.

139
00:19:25,529 --> 00:19:27,820
If there are many layers, this work gets hard.

140
00:19:28,544 --> 00:19:31,461
I'm correcting each layer one by one.

141
00:19:32,469 --> 00:19:35,681
I desperately want to merge the layers quickly.

142
00:20:06,274 --> 00:20:10,590
Let me also do the first-pass coloring on this panel detail.

143
00:20:10,591 --> 00:20:13,991
I plan to make it a material that reflects well, like metal or a mirror.

144
00:20:15,419 --> 00:20:20,350
Since I've added color to the background, it'd be good if it washes onto this panel too.

145
00:20:20,351 --> 00:20:23,511
Sample the background color with the eyedropper and paint.

146
00:20:24,889 --> 00:20:28,940
Then, with the feeling that various objects are reflected, I'll paint a variety of colors.

147
00:20:31,694 --> 00:20:35,480
I'll also mix in complement colors a little.

148
00:20:35,481 --> 00:20:41,720
In a picture made up of similar-toned colors, which can easily get boring, this'll be a bit of a fun element and point.

149
00:20:43,215 --> 00:20:49,281
Since the details and complement contrast are all concentrated on the chest and shoulder area, the focus of attention will be clear.

150
00:21:15,739 --> 00:21:19,550
This time, we did the first-pass coloring.

151
00:21:19,550 --> 00:21:21,870
Next time, I'll continue painting.
